                     /note="Domain:cl28411:COG2932 Phage repressor protein C,
                     contains Cro/C1-type HTH and peptisase s24 domains
                     [Mobilome: prophages, transposons]. LexA acts as a
                     homodimer to repress a number of genes involved in the
                     response to DNA damage (SOS response), including itself and
                     RecA. RecA, in the presence of single-stranded DNA, acts as
                     a co-protease to activate a latent autolytic protease
                     activity (EC 3.4.21.88) of LexA, where the active site Ser
                     is part of LexA. The autolytic cleavage site is an Ala-Gly
                     bond in LexA (at position 84-85 in E. coli LexA; this
                     sequence is replaced by Gly-Gly in Synechocystis). The
                     cleavage leads to derepression of the SOS regulon and
                     eventually to DNA repair. LexA in Bacillus subtilis is
                     called DinR. LexA is much less broadly distributed than
                     RecA. [DNA metabolism, DNA replication, recombination, and
                     repair, Regulatory functions, DNA interactions]
                     [Superfamily, Aligned:31-135, Eval:1.1e-19, score:78.1,
                     N-term missing]"
                     /note="MGA_4126"
